{
  "status": "answered",
  "answer": "When parallel parking on a road with a continuous dividing line, a driver must position their vehicle at least 3 meters from the dividing line (Doc 2). To do this, park your vehicle with the rear of the car aligned with the parked cars on the side of the road, then slowly drive forward while steering the car into the parking space. Ensure there is at least 3 meters of space between the rear of your vehicle and the dividing line.",
  "general_tip": "",
  "evidence": [
    "(6) If the road has a continuous dividing line or a dividing strip, the driver must position the vehicle at least 3m from the continuous dividing line or dividing strip, unless otherwise indicated by information on or with a parking control sign.",
    "(7) If the road does not have a continuous dividing line or a dividing strip, the driver must position the vehicle so there is at least 3m of the road alongside the vehicle that is clear for other vehicles to pass, unless otherwise indicated by information on or with a parking control sign."
  ],
  "sources": [
    "[doc2_s04_c56]"
  ]
}

{
  "status": "answered",
  "answer": "A driver approaching a bridge or length of narrow road must give way to any oncoming vehicle that is on, or approaching, the bridge or length of road when the driver reaches the sign.",
  "general_tip": "Always be prepared to give way to vehicles oncoming from the opposite direction when approaching any type of road obstruction or narrowing, including bridges and narrow sections.",
  "evidence": [
    "70 Giving way at give way sign at bridge or length of narrow road",
    "A driver approaching a bridge or length of narrow road with a give way sign must give way to any oncoming vehicle that is on, or approaching, the bridge or length of road when the driver reaches the sign."
  ],
  "sources": [
    "[doc2_s28_c7]",
    "[doc2_s28_c8]"
  ]
}

{
  "status": "answered",
  "answer": "A driver must drive to the left of a keep left sign.",
  "general_tip": "Always follow the direction indicated by traffic signs and markings to maintain safe driving practices.",
  "evidence": [
    "(1) A driver driving past a keep left sign must drive to the left of the sign.",
    "(2) A driver driving past a keep right sign must drive to the right of the sign."
  ],
  "sources": [
    "[doc2_s00_c9]",
    "[doc2_s00_c10]"
  ]
}

{
  "status": "answered",
  "answer": "No, individuals are not allowed to ride wheeled recreational devices on roads at night. You must only use them during daylight hours unless crossing the road to avoid being caught breaking the rules.",
  "general_tip": "Always check the time of day before using wheeled recreational devices on roads to ensure compliance with the rules.",
  "evidence": [
    "Maximum penalty: 20 penalty units. (Doc 2)",
    "A person must not travel in or on a wheeled recreational device or wheeled toy on a road at night. Maximum penalty: 20 penalty units. (Doc 2)"
  ],
  "sources": [
    "[doc2_s06_c23]",
    "[doc2_s06_c23]"
  ]
}

{
  "status": "answered",
  "answer": "Anything used on or with a traffic control device or traffic-related item, such as words, figures, symbols, or other markings, has the same meaning as specified in the ACT Road Rules. This includes the specific definitions and interpretations provided for each type of symbol or marking.",
  "general_tip": "Symbols and markings on traffic control devices and traffic-related items must comply with the specific regulations outlined in the ACT Road Rules to ensure clarity and effectiveness for road users.",
  "evidence": [
    "(3) A symbol on a traffic-related item may be a reasonable likeness of a diagram of a kind of symbol mentioned in this regulation even though the dimensions of the symbol, or of anything on the symbol, are different.",
    "(321) Meaning of information on or with traffic control device and traffic-related item"
  ],
  "sources": [
    "[doc3_s05_c15]",
    "[doc3_s05_c13]"
  ]
}

{
  "status": "answered",
  "answer": "In a built-up area, the default speed limit is 50km/h.",
  "general_tip": "If you're unsure about the speed limit in a built-up area, it's best to assume it's 50km/h unless signs indicate otherwise.",
  "evidence": [
    "(2) The default speed limit applying to a driver for a length of road in a built-up area is 50km/h."
  ],
  "sources": [
    "[doc1_s24_c2]"
  ]
}

{
  "status": "answered",
  "answer": "No, you should not start crossing the intersection when the traffic lights are red. Wait until the lights change to green or flashing yellow, or there is no red light showing, before you start crossing.",
  "general_tip": "Always look both ways and check the traffic lights before crossing an intersection to ensure it is safe.",
  "evidence": [
    "If the traffic lights show a red or yellow traffic light and the pedestrian has not already started crossing the intersection or road, the pedestrian must not start to cross until the traffic lights change to green or flashing yellow, or there is no red traffic light showing. (Doc 2)",
    "If, while the pedestrian is crossing the road, the traffic lights change to yellow or red, the pedestrian must not stay on the road for longer than necessary to cross safely to the nearer (in the direction of travel of the pedestrian) of the following: (a) a dividing strip, safety zone, or traffic island, forming part of the area set aside or used by pedestrians to cross the road at the intersection or place (the safety area); (b) the far side of the road. (Doc 2)"
  ],
  "sources": [
    "[doc2_s06_c3]",
    "[doc2_s06_c4]"
  ]
}
